{
  "rationale": "The spec is low-area with a loose throughput constraint (>=1 MSPS) and a moderate accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-10). Both iterative and unrolled_k can easily meet the throughput constraint: even at a modest Fmax, iterative with N+3 cycles yields >1 MSPS for N up to ~30. Area is the primary objective, so we focus on the family with the smallest datapath: iterative shares one micro-rotation per cycle, minimising hardware. unrolled_k replicates k micro-rotations per cycle, increasing area, so it is likely dominated by iterative for the same accuracy. However, we include unrolled_k with a small budget to confirm and to catch any unexpected trade-off (e.g., if iterative has control overhead that makes a small-k unrolled version competitive). The accuracy constraint requires roughly W >= 12 and N >= 11, with angle_guard >= -1 and possibly frac_guard >= 1 to meet the error bound. We therefore focus the search on the low end of data_width (11..14) and n_iter (10..14), with angle_guard from -2 to 1 and frac_guard 0..2, and both rounding modes. This range brackets the minimum-area designs that just meet the accuracy constraint, which is where the Pareto front will start. We allocate 85% of the budget to iterative and 15% to unrolled_k.",
